--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_001.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_001.json
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0205-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0205-complete-cayley-spectrum",
+      "namespace": "probability.markov",
+      "logical_name": "complete-cayley-geometric-target-metropolis-spectrum",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Exact complete-Cayley geometric-target Metropolis relaxation [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "Let G have order n>=2 and identity e. For 0<alpha<=1 let P(x,x)=1-alpha and P(x,y)=alpha/(n-1) for y!=x. Start P at e and stop at independent T with P(T=k)=p(1-p)^k, k>=0, 0<p<1. Put b=1-alpha*n/(n-1) and a=p/(1-(1-p)*b). The Metropolis kernel M with proposal P and target Law(X_T) has spectral-gap relaxation time tau(M)=tau(P)*(1+(n-1)*a), where tau(P)=(n-1)/(alpha*n). It is strictly increasing in p. With alpha=p=1/2 both kernels are lazy and tau(M)/[n*tau(P)] tends to 2/3 as n tends to infinity. This family has proposal degree n-1 and does not settle a fixed-degree variant or a differently parametrised monotonicity question.",
+      "proof_text": "P acts as the scalar b on the space orthogonal to constants. Summing p*sum_k (1-p)^k P^k gives the displayed target. Every proposal from a nonidentity state is accepted. From e to any other state, its probability is alpha/(n-1) times r=(1-a)/(1+(n-1)*a). Thus the two-state radial lump has rates alpha*r and alpha/(n-1), and nonconstant eigenvalue 1-alpha*r-alpha/(n-1). The n-2 contrasts between nonidentity states have eigenvalue b. Since 0<a<1 and r<1, the radial eigenvalue is at least b and is lambda_2. Its gap is alpha*(r+1/(n-1))=alpha*n/((n-1)*(1+(n-1)*a)). Inversion proves the formula, including n=2 where the contrast subspace is empty. Differentiating a=p/(1-b+b*p) gives a prime=(1-b)/(1-b+b*p)^2>0. For alpha=p=1/2, a=(n-1)/(3*n/2-1), so (1+(n-1)*a)/n tends to 2/3.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Finite group order n>=2",
+        "Uniform nonidentity proposal with holding 1-alpha",
+        "Geo0 convention and inverse ordinary spectral gap"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0205-complete-cayley-spectrum",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0205_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0205-complete-cayley-spectrum",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0205-metropolis-relaxation-for-geometric-time-cayley-targets",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This family has proposal degree n-1\"; existing card: \"Control the relaxation time of Metropolis random walk targeting the distribution of a Cayley walk at an independent geometric time; in particular, test monotonic decrease in the geometric parameter\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0205-complete-cayley-spectrum",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:attempt-p0205-metropolis-relaxation-for-geometric-time-cayley-targets",
+      "kind": "generalizes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"tau(M)=tau(P)*(1+(n-1)*a)\"; existing card: \"Monotone decrease is false already on the two-element Cayley group\"."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_002.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_002.json
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0429-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0429-path-collision-obstruction",
+      "namespace": "probability.queueing",
+      "logical_name": "uniform-batch-arrival-path-cubic-collision-obstruction",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Sharp cubic collision scale for batch arrival paths [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "For n servers, Poisson jobs of rate lambda*n/k routing to uniform k-subsets, and k tagged arrival streams observed exactly on [0,T], let H count tagged servers hit per job and beta=(lambda*n/k)*P(H>=2). Their joint path total variation from independent rate-lambda Poisson streams is at least 1-exp(-beta*T). If k tends to infinity with k=o(sqrt(n)), beta~lambda*k^3/(2*n). At k/n^(1/3)->c>0, the liminf is at least 1-exp(-lambda*T*c^3/2). Conversely k=o(n^(1/3)) gives path TV tending to zero by the delete-and-restore coupling. This proves a path-coupling obstruction, not a necessary threshold for a stationary queue vector at a single time.",
+      "proof_text": "The total variation distance between the joint arrival-path law and k independent rate-lambda Poisson paths is at least 1-exp(-beta*T). Indeed the observed event that two tagged paths have a simultaneous arrival has probability 1-exp(-beta*T) in the original model by Poisson thinning, and zero under independent Poisson streams. Arrival times are continuous and the interval is observed exactly.\n\nThe factorial moments are E[(H)_r]=(k)_r^2/(n)_r. For nonnegative integer H,\n\n    E[(H)_2]/2 - E[(H)_3]/2 <= P(H>=2) <= E[(H)_2]/2.\n\nThe upper inequality is the pair union bound. The lower follows pointwise from binom(h,2)-1_{h>=2} <= (h)_3/2 (check h=0,1,2, and for h>=3 use h-2>=1). If k grows and k=o(sqrt(n)), then E[(H)_3]/E[(H)_2]=(k-2)^2/(n-2)->0. Consequently beta ~ lambda*k^3/(2*n). In particular if k/n^(1/3)->c>0, the path-TV liminf is at least 1-exp(-lambda*T*c^3/2)>0. If n^(1/3)<<k<<sqrt(n), this lower bound tends to one.\n\nFor completeness, delete original jobs hitting at least two tagged servers and restore each lost marginal arrival rate with independent Poisson streams. The remaining one-tag streams are independent by Poisson marking; the restored marginal rates yield k independent rate-lambda streams. A disagreement probability is at most Lambda*T*(P(H>=2)+E[H*1_{H>=2}]) <= (3/2)*Lambda*T*E[(H)_2] = 3*lambda*T*k*(k-1)^2/(2*(n-1)). Hence k=o(n^(1/3)) suffices at fixed T. The cubic scale is therefore sharp for this exact-path coupling in the stated growing-k regime. Passing from path-TV to the stationary queue vector loses the simultaneous-time witness; no necessary stationary-vector threshold follows from this proof.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Poisson jobs with independent uniform routing",
+        "lambda,T fixed and positive",
+        "Exact continuous arrival paths",
+        "For the asymptotic formula: k tends to infinity and k=o(sqrt(n))"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0429-path-collision-obstruction",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0429_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0429-path-collision-obstruction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0429-largest-growing-fork-join-batch-compatible-with-asymptotic-independenc",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This proves a path-coupling obstruction\"; existing card: \"Determine how quickly the number k(n) of tasks per uniformly routed job can grow while the corresponding k(n) stationary queues remain asymptotically independent\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0429-path-collision-obstruction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:attempt-p0429-largest-growing-fork-join-batch-compatible-with-asymptotic-independenc",
+      "kind": "generalizes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"beta~lambda*k^3/(2*n)\"; existing card: \"A finite-horizon input coupling suggests a cubic collision scale\"."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_003.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_003.json
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-c0127-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "c0127-common-factor-peaks",
+      "namespace": "probability.gaussian",
+      "logical_name": "common-gaussian-factor-growing-near-peaks-without-superconcentration",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Common-factor obstruction to a growing-peaks criterion [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "Fix 0<rho<1 and independent standard normal Z_0,...,Z_m. Let X_i=sqrt(rho)*Z_0+sqrt(1-rho)*Z_i and M_m=max_i X_i. Coordinates have variance one and distinct covariance rho. Var(M_m)>=rho, so the field is not superconcentrated. Nevertheless the number of coordinates at deficit at most delta_m=(log m)^(-1/4) from M_m tends to infinity in probability. Thus growing near-peaks with pairwise covariance merely bounded away from one, even at vanishing absolute deficit, are insufficient for superconcentration. This does not refute a criterion requiring overlaps to tend to zero.",
+      "proof_text": "Fix 0<rho<1. For m>=2 let Z_0,Z_1,...,Z_m be independent standard normal variables, and X_i=sqrt(rho)*Z_0+sqrt(1-rho)*Z_i. Each X_i has variance one; distinct coordinates have covariance rho, bounded away from one. Put M_m=max_i X_i. Independence gives\n\n    Var(M_m)=rho+(1-rho)*Var(max_i Z_i)>=rho.\n\nThus these maxima are not superconcentrated relative to the unit coordinate variance. Any two selected coordinates have covariance at least rho, including maximisers before and after Gaussian noise, so an overlap-to-zero chaos criterion also fails.\n\nNevertheless, for delta_m=(log m)^(-1/4), the number K_m of coordinates within delta_m of M_m tends to infinity in probability. To prove this, let b_m=sqrt(2 log m), d_m=delta_m/(2 sqrt(1-rho)), and x_m=b_m-d_m. A Gaussian Chernoff bound gives\n\n    P(max_i Z_i > b_m+d_m) <= exp(-b_m*d_m-d_m^2/2) -> 0.\n\nLet N_m count Z_i>=x_m. It is binomial with mean mu_m=m*barPhi(x_m). For x>=1, integrating the Gaussian density on [x,x+1/x] gives barPhi(x)>=exp(-3/2)*exp(-x^2/2)/(sqrt(2*pi)*x). Therefore\n\n    log mu_m >= b_m*d_m-d_m^2/2-log(x_m)-3/2-log(2*pi)/2 -> infinity.\n\nHere b_m*d_m is a positive constant times (log m)^(1/4), which dominates log(x_m)=O(log log m). Since Var(N_m)<=mu_m, Chebyshev gives N_m->infinity in probability. On the high-probability maximum event, each of these N_m coordinates is within 2*d_m of max Z, hence within delta_m of M_m. Thus K_m>=N_m with probability tending to one.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Independent standard Gaussian coordinates",
+        "Fixed positive common covariance rho",
+        "Candidate uses overlap bounded away from one, not overlap tending to zero"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0127-common-factor-peaks",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0127_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "c0127-two-peak-quantifier",
+      "namespace": "probability.gaussian",
+      "logical_name": "two-independent-gaussian-peaks-fixed-deficit-probability",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Two-peak counterexample needs a probability quantifier [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "For independent standard normal Z_1,Z_2 and fixed d>0, the probability both coordinates are within d of max(Z_1,Z_2) equals erf(d/2)<1. Repeating coordinates in two blocks does not change this probability. Such replication alone cannot establish near-maximal low-overlap peaks with probability tending to one.",
+      "proof_text": "Both coordinates are within d exactly when |Z_1-Z_2|<=d. Their difference is N(0,2), giving erf(d/2). Its Gaussian tail is strictly positive for finite d.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Fixed finite deficit d",
+        "Two independent standard normal block values"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0127-two-peak-quantifier",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0127_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0127-common-factor-peaks",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.gaussian:open-problem-c0127-chaos-versus-superconcentration-an-equivalent-multiple-peaks-condition",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This does not refute a criterion requiring overlaps to tend to zero\"; existing card: \"Find a multiple-peaks condition on a Gaussian field that is equivalent to chaos and superconcentration, or show that no such condition exists\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0127-common-factor-peaks",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.gaussian:open-problem-c0127-chaos-versus-superconcentration-an-equivalent-multiple-peaks-condition",
+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"Var(M_m)>=rho\"; existing card: \"Find a multiple-peaks condition on a Gaussian field that is equivalent to chaos and superconcentration, or show that no such condition exists\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0127-two-peak-quantifier",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.gaussian:open-problem-c0127-chaos-versus-superconcentration-an-equivalent-multiple-peaks-condition",
+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"erf(d/2)<1\"; existing card: \"Find a multiple-peaks condition on a Gaussian field that is equivalent to chaos and superconcentration, or show that no such condition exists\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_004.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_004.json
@@ -0,0 +1,71 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0050-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0050-square-gap-singularity",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-graphs",
+      "logical_name": "negative-correlation-divergent-squared-marginal-gap-singularity",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Singularity from a divergent squared marginal gap [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "Let mu and nu be laws of countably many zero-one indicators X_i, pairwise negatively correlated under each law. Suppose delta_i=E_mu X_i-E_nu X_i>=0 and sum_i delta_i^2=infinity. Then mu and nu are mutually singular. The criterion needs no common invariant group action. Its application to distinct free and wired spanning-forest laws requires checking the ordered-marginal, negative-correlation and divergent-squared-gap hypotheses; that divergence is not proved for arbitrary networks here.",
+      "proof_text": "For S_N=sum_{i<=N} delta_i X_i and D_N=sum_{i<=N} delta_i^2, the difference of expectations is D_N. Nonnegative weights and nonpositive off-diagonal covariances imply\n\n    Var_mu(S_N), Var_nu(S_N) <= sum_i delta_i^2 Var(X_i) <= D_N/4.\n\nLet t_N be the midpoint of the two means. Chebyshev gives mu(S_N<=t_N)<=1/D_N and nu(S_N>t_N)<=1/D_N. Choose increasing N_j with D_{N_j}>=2^j. Both error sums are finite. The first Borel-Cantelli lemma therefore makes the measurable event \"S_{N_j}>t_{N_j} for all sufficiently large j\" have mu-probability one and nu-probability zero. This is mutual singularity.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Countable indicator coordinates",
+        "Pairwise nonpositive covariances under both laws",
+        "Ordered marginal probabilities",
+        "Divergent sum of squared marginal differences"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0050-square-gap-singularity",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0050_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "p0050-finite-gap-boundary",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-graphs",
+      "logical_name": "finite-coordinate-bernoulli-change-equivalent-measures",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Finite marginal differences need not imply singularity [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "The independent Bernoulli product laws with first-coordinate parameters 3/5 and 2/5 and all other parameters 1/2 are distinct but equivalent. Their squared marginal-gap sum is 1/25. This example shows the preceding sufficient criterion cannot discard its divergence hypothesis or replace it by distinctness alone.",
+      "proof_text": "Only the first-coordinate density changes. The Radon-Nikodym derivative is 3/2 on X_1=1 and 2/3 on X_1=0, strictly positive and finite everywhere.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Independent Bernoulli coordinates",
+        "Exactly one changed coordinate"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0050-finite-gap-boundary",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0050_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0050-square-gap-singularity",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0050-singularity-of-distinct-free-and-wired-forest-measures",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"The criterion needs no common invariant group action\"; existing card: \"If the wired and free uniform spanning-forest measures differ on an infinite network, must they be mutually singular? Source: Probability on Trees and Networks, paperback electronic version 19 August 2026, Question 10.58, printed p.385.\nPosed in Probability on Trees and Networks,\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0050-square-gap-singularity",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:attempt-p0050-singularity-of-distinct-free-and-wired-forest-measures",
+      "kind": "analogous_to",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"sum_i delta_i^2=infinity\"; existing card: \"Arbitrary networks may lack it\"."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0050-finite-gap-boundary",
+      "to_key": "p0050-square-gap-singularity",
+      "kind": "semantic_association",
+      "rationale": "Boundary card: \"squared marginal-gap sum is 1/25\"; criterion card: \"sum_i delta_i^2=infinity\". The boundary prevents dropping the divergence hypothesis."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_005.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_005.json
@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0255-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0255-weighted-star-cv",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-graphs",
+      "logical_name": "weighted-star-nearest-unvisited-walk-coefficient-of-variation-bound",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "An absolute variation bound for weighted-star NUV walks [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "For a finite star with at least one leaf and positive edge lengths, and a nearest-unvisited walk that stops after all vertices are visited, every random start distribution satisfies sd(L)/E L<=1/(2*sqrt(6)). For at least two leaves, write S for the sum of all edge lengths and w_1>=w_2 for the largest two. All lengths lie in [2S-w_1-w_2,2S-w_1], whose endpoint ratio is at most 3/2. A one-leaf star has constant positive length and coefficient zero. The constant is sharp over arbitrary start distributions; the uniform-start case is included but no optimality claim for that subclass is made. This does not prove an absolute bound for arbitrary graphs.",
+      "proof_text": "Consider a star with m>=2 leaves, positive leaf-edge lengths w_i, total S, and largest two lengths w_1>=w_2. A nearest-unvisited walk stops when every vertex has been visited; it does not return to its start. Its length from the centre is B=2S-w_1. From leaf r it first visits the centre and then the remaining leaves in increasing edge-length order, giving L_r=2S-w_r-max_{j!=r} w_j. Ties between equal weights do not change the total length.\n\nHence every start length lies between A=2S-w_1-w_2 and B. Since S>=w_1+w_2 and w_1>=w_2, B>=3w_2 and B/A<=3/2. For any random start, not only the uniform law, if u=E L then averaging (L-A)(B-L)>=0 gives Var(L)<=(B-u)(u-A). Further,\n\n    (B-A)^2/(4AB) - (B-u)(u-A)/u^2\n      = ((A+B)*u-2AB)^2/(4AB*u^2) >= 0.\n\nThus sd(L)/E L <= (B-A)/(2 sqrt(AB)) <= 1/(2 sqrt(6)). A one-leaf star has constant length and ratio zero. This proves an absolute bound for the weighted-star class requested as a boundary case of P0255.\n\nThe constant is sharp if arbitrary start distributions are allowed: on the three-vertex star with both weights one, give the centre probability 2/5 and each leaf probability 3/10. Then L is three with probability 2/5 and two with probability 3/5, giving Var(L)/(E L)^2=1/24. Under the uniform law that same star has coefficient sqrt(2)/7; no claim of optimality within the uniform-start subclass is made.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Positive weighted star with at least one leaf",
+        "Walk stops on visiting the final new vertex",
+        "Arbitrary random starting vertex"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0255-weighted-star-cv",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0255_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0255-weighted-star-cv",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0255-variation-of-nearest-unvisited-walk-length-under-a-random-start",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This does not prove an absolute bound for arbitrary graphs\"; existing card: \"For every finite positive-length graph G and a uniform initial vertex V, is sd(L_NUV(G,V))/E L_NUV(G,V) bounded by an absolute constant? Source: Aldous: The Nearest Unvisited Vertex Walk on Random Graphs,internal date26August2021, Conjecture5 and stronger Open Problem4,PDF8. Stat\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0255-weighted-star-cv",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:attempt-p0255-variation-of-nearest-unvisited-walk-length-under-a-random-start",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"endpoint ratio is at most 3/2\"; existing card: \"a rigorous O(sqrt(log n)) coefficient-of-variation bound follows\"."
+    }
+  ]
+}
