@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-c0014-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "c0014-wiring-direction",
+      "namespace": "probability.electrical-networks",
+      "logical_name": "wired-layer-resistance-lower-comparison-and-interface-current-obstruction",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Wired interfaces reverse the unsupported layer upper comparison [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "In a finite resistor network whose contracted intermediate interfaces yield a series chain of independently wired layer networks, the sum of those wired layer resistances is at most the original effective resistance. Rayleigh monotonicity alone cannot justify the opposite upper bound. On s-a-t and s-b-t with resistances (1,M) and (M,1), respectively, the original resistance is (M+1)/2 and the wired layer sum is 2M/(M+1). Their ratio (M+1)^2/(4M) is unbounded. To upper-bound the original resistance using layer flows, the interface current distributions must match. This does not identify a DGFF limiting law or refute an upper bound constructed using matched flows.",
+      "proof_text": "For a finite positive-resistance network separated into successive layers by interfaces, identify all vertices in each intermediate interface and replace the boundary terminal sets by wired terminals. If the resulting quotient is a series chain of wired layer networks, its total effective resistance is the sum of their wired effective resistances. Rayleigh monotonicity gives\n\n    sum of wired layer resistances <= original effective resistance.\n\nIdentification introduces extra zero-cost connections and decreases effective resistance. It does not, by itself, give the reverse upper bound. For an upper bound by Thomson's principle, unit flows in consecutive layers must have matching distributions on the intermediate interface.\n\nAn explicit counterexample has vertices s,a,b,t and resistances r(s,a)=r(b,t)=1, r(a,t)=r(s,b)=M>0. Before wiring a to b there are two parallel paths, each of resistance M+1, so R_original=(M+1)/2. Wiring a and b gives two successive parallel pairs with resistance M/(M+1), so their sum is R_wired=2M/(M+1). The ratio is (M+1)^2/(4M), unbounded as M grows. At M=100, R_original=50.5 and R_wired=200/101, a factor 25.5025 smaller.\n\nThe independently optimal wired-layer unit flows have incompatible interface currents. In the first layer the low-resistance edge into a carries M/(M+1); in the second layer the edge leaving a carries 1/(M+1). Their mismatch is (M-1)/(M+1). Joining these minimisers as a flow in the original graph violates Kirchhoff's law unless M=1. Wiring supplies the missing current transfer artificially.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Finite positive-resistance network",
+        "Wired layer terminal minima",
+        "Contracted quotient is a series chain"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0014-wiring-direction",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0014_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0014-wiring-direction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.gaussian:open-problem-c0014-limit-of-the-normalised-log-effective-resistance-of-the-dgff-electric",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This does not identify a DGFF limiting law\"; existing card: \"For admissible approximations D_N of a domain D and the network with resistances (13.13) built from the DGFF, characterise the limit distribution of x -> (log N)^{-1/2} log R_eff(floor(xN), D_N^c) as N -> infinity\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0014-wiring-direction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.gaussian:attempt-c0014-limit-of-the-normalised-log-effective-resistance-of-the-dgff-electric",
+      "kind": "refutes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"Rayleigh monotonicity alone cannot justify the opposite upper bound\"; existing card: \"Rayleigh monotonicity gives R_eff(x, D_N^c) <= sum over dyadic annuli around x of the resistance across the annulus\"."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_007.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_007.json
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-c0073-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "c0073-speed-measure-obstruction",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-walks",
+      "logical_name": "dangling-leaf-conductance-constant-speed-diffusion-normalization",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Walk clocks change the conductivity-to-diffusion comparison [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "On the integer spine with unit conductances and a conductance-a dangling leaf at each site, a>0, project both vertices in a cell to its integer coordinate. The rate-one constant-speed walk has variance growth D_CS=1/(1+a), whereas the edge-rate variable-speed walk has D_VS=1. Electrical conductivity per cell is one for every a. The speed masses are respectively 2+2a and 2, so increasing leaf conductance slows the constant-speed walk despite nondecreasing conductivity. This is a periodic-network normalization example, not a counterexample to iid high-dimensional percolation monotonicity or its proposed critical exponent.",
+      "proof_text": "For the continuous-time constant-speed walk (total jump rate one), the transitions from a spine vertex are left and right at rate 1/(2+a) each and to its leaf at rate a/(2+a); from a leaf the return rate is one. The two-type stationary spine probability is (2+a)/(2+2a), by balance. The projected coordinate is a martingale and its quadratic-variation rate is 2/(2+a) at a spine vertex and zero at a leaf. Thus\n\n    D_CS = [(2+a)/(2+2a)] * [2/(2+a)] = 1/(1+a).\n\nIn stationary type, the variance identity holds exactly for every t. Ergodicity of the two-state type chain gives the same limiting growth from any initial type, and bounded martingale jumps yield the invariance-principle variance by the martingale central-limit argument.\n\nFor the variable-speed walk with each edge jump rate equal to its conductance, the spine-to-leaf and leaf-to-spine rates are both a. The stationary spine fraction is 1/2 and the projected quadratic-variation rate there is two, hence D_VS=1 for every a>0.\n\nThe electrical conductivity per spatial cell is kappa=1: all macroscopic current uses the spine and no current flows into a dangling leaf. The constant-speed speed-measure mass per cell is m_CS=2+2a, while the variable-speed counting mass is two. Therefore D_CS=2*kappa/m_CS and D_VS=2*kappa/2. Increasing a leaves kappa unchanged and strictly decreases D_CS. Rayleigh monotonicity of conductivity cannot, without a separate speed-measure argument, imply monotonicity of a normalized walk diffusion coefficient.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Periodic decorated integer spine",
+        "Positive dangling conductance a",
+        "Diffusion means coordinate variance growth per unit time",
+        "Specified constant-speed or edge-rate clock"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0073-speed-measure-obstruction",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0073_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0073-speed-measure-obstruction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.percolation:open-problem-c0073-critical-exponent-of-the-diffusion-constant-of-random-walk-on-the-supe",
+      "kind": "analogous_to",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This is a periodic-network normalization example\"; existing card: \"For random walk on the infinite percolation cluster in sufficiently high dimension, show that the diffusion constant satisfies sigma(p)^2 ~ (p - p_c)^2 as p decreases to p_c; even monotonicity of p -> sigma^2(p) is not known\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0073-speed-measure-obstruction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.percolation:open-problem-c0073-critical-exponent-of-the-diffusion-constant-of-random-walk-on-the-supe",
+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"increasing leaf conductance slows the constant-speed walk\"; existing card: \"For random walk on the infinite percolation cluster in sufficiently high dimension, show that the diffusion constant satisfies sigma(p)^2 ~ (p - p_c)^2 as p decreases to p_c; even monotonicity of p -> sigma^2(p) is not known\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_008.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_008.json
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-c0109-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "c0109-truncation-envelope",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-matrices",
+      "logical_name": "finite-two-plus-epsilon-moment-tail-variance-envelope-sharp-power",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Moment truncation controls variance without proving a spectral rate [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "If eps>0 and M=E|X|^(2+eps)<infinity, then E[|X|^2 1_{|X|>R}]<=M*R^(-eps) for R>0. The power eps is sharp as a scalar envelope: a symmetric atom law with total mass c*2^(-(2+eps)*j)/j^2 at +/-2^j has finite (2+eps)-moment and tail variance of order 2^(-eps*m)/m^2 at R=2^m. This proves neither a lower bound nor an optimal exponent for a circular-law convergence rate.",
+      "proof_text": "For eps>0, R>0 and M=E|X|^(2+eps)<infinity,\n\n    E[|X|^2 * 1_{|X|>R}] <= M*R^(-eps),\n    P(|X|>R) <= M*R^(-(2+eps)).\n\nThese follow pointwise from |X|^2<=R^(-eps)|X|^(2+eps) on the tail, and Markov's inequality. At R=n^r the first bound is M*n^(-r*eps). It bounds a truncation error; it is not a lower bound for the error of the original matrix's empirical eigenvalue law.\n\nThe power eps in the scalar tail envelope cannot be uniformly increased under the same moment assumption. Take a symmetric law with total probability q_j=c*2^(-(2+eps)*j)/j^2 at the two atoms +/-2^j, j>=1, and remaining mass at zero, choosing c>0 sufficiently small. It has mean zero, positive finite variance and finite (2+eps)-moment c*sum_j j^(-2). At R=2^m, its missing variance is\n\n    c*sum_{j>m} 2^(-eps*j)/j^2.\n\nThe first summand gives a lower bound; a geometric series after replacing j by m gives an upper bound. For m>=1 this quantity lies between positive constants times 2^(-eps*m)/m^2. Thus it is not O(R^(-eps-eta)) for any eta>0 along dyadic cutoffs. Rescaling by its standard deviation preserves the tail exponent. This sharpness statement concerns a scalar truncation envelope and does not prove a moment-dependent circular-law lower bound.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "eps>0",
+        "Finite (2+eps)-moment",
+        "Tail sharpness concerns scalar laws only"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0109-truncation-envelope",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0109_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "c0109-nonnormal-rank-obstruction",
+      "namespace": "probability.random-matrices",
+      "logical_name": "rank-one-nonnormal-perturbation-unit-spectral-measure-discrepancy",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Rank-one perturbations can move a nonnormal spectral law by one [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "For n>=2, let J_n be the nilpotent matrix with ones on the superdiagonal, and C_n=J_n+e_n e_1^T. Their difference has rank one, but the empirical eigenvalue laws differ by one on the bounded 1-Lipschitz test f(z)=min(|z|,1): J_n has only zero eigenvalues and C_n has all nth roots of unity. Thus a Hermitian-style rank/n bound cannot control nonnormal empirical eigenvalue laws in general. This deterministic example is not an iid circular-law lower bound.",
+      "proof_text": "J_n^n=0. For every z with z^n=1, C_n(1,z,...,z^(n-1))=z(1,z,...,z^(n-1)); these are n distinct eigenvalues. The perturbation is the single nonzero outer product e_n e_1^T. The radial test is zero at the origin and one at each root of unity.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Deterministic nonnormal matrices",
+        "n>=2",
+        "Bounded Lipschitz comparison of eigenvalue laws"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:c0109-nonnormal-rank-obstruction",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/C0109_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0109-truncation-envelope",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.random-matrices:open-problem-c0109-rate-of-convergence-in-the-circular-law-as-a-function-of-the-moment-as",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This proves neither a lower bound nor an optimal exponent\"; existing card: \"Determine the optimal rate n^{-delta} of convergence of the empirical spectral distribution of n^{-1/2} M_n to the circular law under a (2+eps)-th moment assumption, and the exact dependence of delta on eps\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0109-truncation-envelope",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.random-matrices:attempt-c0109-rate-of-convergence-in-the-circular-law-as-a-function-of-the-moment-as",
+      "kind": "cites",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"tail variance of order 2^(-eps*m)/m^2\"; existing card: \"which caps delta(eps)\"."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0109-nonnormal-rank-obstruction",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.random-matrices:open-problem-c0109-rate-of-convergence-in-the-circular-law-as-a-function-of-the-moment-as",
+      "kind": "analogous_to",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This deterministic example is not an iid circular-law lower bound\"; existing card: \"Determine the optimal rate n^{-delta} of convergence of the empirical spectral distribution of n^{-1/2} M_n to the circular law under a (2+eps)-th moment assumption, and the exact dependence of delta on eps\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "c0109-nonnormal-rank-obstruction",
+      "to_key": "c0109-truncation-envelope",
+      "kind": "semantic_association",
+      "rationale": "Rank card: \"a Hermitian-style rank/n bound cannot control nonnormal empirical eigenvalue laws\"; tail card: \"This proves neither a lower bound nor an optimal exponent\". Both limit unsupported transfers in a truncation route."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_009.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_009.json
@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0004-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0004-nonstationary-tree",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "bounded-degree-tree-without-rooted-connective-limit",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Bounded degree alone does not give a connective limit [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "There is an infinite connected tree of maximum degree three whose rooted self-avoiding-walk counts c_n satisfy liminf c_n^(1/n)=1 and limsup c_n^(1/n)=2. Use successive level-block lengths 2^(2^j), with one child in even blocks and two in odd blocks. Rooted scalar submultiplicativity already fails at c_1=c_2=1,c_3=2. This refutes a generic bounded-degree existence route, but the example is not a stationary iid percolation cluster of Z^2.",
+      "proof_text": "Let c_n(o) count length-n self-avoiding walks from the root of an infinite spherically symmetric tree. At level l, all vertices have b_l children. Partition the levels into successive blocks of lengths L_j=2^(2^j), j=0,1,... . Give one child at every level in even blocks and two children in odd blocks. This is an infinite connected tree with maximum degree three.\n\nEvery self-avoiding root walk moves outward, so c_n(o)=product_{l<n} b_l. At the end N_j=sum_{h<=j} L_h, write B_j for the number of two-child levels among those N_j. Then log(c_{N_j}(o))/(N_j*log 2)=B_j/N_j. The earlier blocks have total at most j*L_{j-1}=j*sqrt(L_j), negligible relative to L_j. Along odd block ends this ratio tends to one; along even block ends it tends to zero. Since every ratio lies in [0,1],\n\n    liminf c_n(o)^(1/n)=1,  limsup c_n(o)^(1/n)=2.\n\nThe exponential limit therefore does not exist even on an infinite connected bounded-degree graph. On the first three levels, c_1=c_2=1 but c_3=2, directly refuting scalar rooted submultiplicativity c_{n+m}(o)<=c_n(o)*c_m(o). This tree lacks the translation-stationary percolation structure of P0004 and is not a counterexample to that problem.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Infinite spherically symmetric rooted tree",
+        "Nonstationary alternating branching blocks",
+        "Rooted self-avoiding-walk counts"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0004-nonstationary-tree",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0004_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "p0004-full-lattice-boundary",
+      "namespace": "probability.percolation",
+      "logical_name": "full-square-lattice-connective-limit-submultiplicativity-boundary",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Translation invariance proves the full-lattice boundary limit [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "For the p=1 bond-percolation configuration on Z^2, let c_n(v) count length-n self-avoiding walks from v. Translation invariance and prefix splitting give c_{n+m}(0)<=c_n(0)*c_m(0). Thus c_n(v)^(1/n) has a common limit in [2,3] by Fekete subadditivity, independent of v. This boundary result does not prove the quenched limit for p in (p_c,1).",
+      "proof_text": "Translate each prefix endpoint to zero; the permitted suffixes inject into all length-m origin walks. This gives scalar submultiplicativity. Fekete applied to log c_n gives the limit. There are 2^n monotone northeast walks and at most 4*3^(n-1) walks without immediate reversal, giving the bounds. Translation preserves the counts.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "p=1 on the square lattice",
+        "Finite-length self-avoiding walks"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0004-full-lattice-boundary",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0004_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    }
+  ],
+  "relations": [
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0004-nonstationary-tree",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0004-existence-of-the-quenched-connective-limit",
+      "kind": "analogous_to",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"the example is not a stationary iid percolation cluster of Z^2\"; existing card: \"On the infinite supercritical bond-percolation cluster of Z^2, does c_n(v)^(1/n) converge almost surely, independently of v? Source: Selected problems in probability theory, Question 2.3, PDF pages 3-4.\nPosed in Selected problems in probability theory\nStatus as recorded in the ca\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0004-nonstationary-tree",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:attempt-p0004-existence-of-the-quenched-connective-limit",
+      "kind": "semantic_association",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"Rooted scalar submultiplicativity already fails\"; existing card: \"No stationary scalar subadditive process or liminf lower bound was obtained\"."
+    },
+    {
+      "from_key": "p0004-full-lattice-boundary",
+      "to_existing_logical": "math:probability.core:open-problem-p0004-existence-of-the-quenched-connective-limit",
+      "kind": "specializes",
+      "rationale": "New card: \"This boundary result does not prove the quenched limit\"; existing card: \"On the infinite supercritical bond-percolation cluster of Z^2, does c_n(v)^(1/n) converge almost surely, independently of v? Source: Selected problems in probability theory, Question 2.3, PDF pages 3-4.\nPosed in Selected problems in probability theory\nStatus as recorded in the ca\". This result treats an explicitly stated special case or limitation; it does not certify a solution."
+    }
+  ]
+}
--- a/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_010.json
+++ b/math-brain-inbox/w335/bundle_010.json
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+{
+  "spec": "mathbrain-draft-v1",
+  "author_agent": "w335",
+  "bundle_slug": "w335-p0027-20261010",
+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "key": "p0027-smooth-map-rigidity",
+      "namespace": "probability.stochastic-processes",
+      "logical_name": "smooth-deterministic-reflected-brownian-state-map-euclidean-rigidity",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Smooth deterministic reflected-Brownian maps are Euclidean isometries [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "Let D be a bounded connected C^2 domain in R^d and F:D->D a C^2 diffeomorphism. If, for every initial x in D, normal reflected Brownian motion X started at x has F(X_t) with the normal reflected-Brownian law started at F(x), then F(x)=Qx+b for a fixed orthogonal Q and b. The conclusion uses smoothness, an onto map, and all starting points; it does not classify arbitrary measurable deterministic maps or convert every shy coupling to a deterministic one.",
+      "proof_text": "At an interior starting point the small-time covariance of F(X_t)-F(x), divided by t, tends to DF(x)*DF(x)^T by Ito's formula (reflection has no first-order effect before the boundary is hit). The corresponding limit for a reflected Brownian motion started at the interior point F(x) is the identity. Thus DF*DF^T=I at every x. Since DF is square, it is orthogonal, so its columns e_i=partial_i F form an orthonormal basis.\n\nSet A_ijk=<partial_i partial_j F,e_k>. Equality of mixed partials gives A_ijk=A_jik. Differentiating <e_j,e_k>=delta_jk gives A_ijk=-A_ikj. Combining the two symmetries,\n\n    A_ijk = A_jik = -A_jki = -A_kji\n           = A_kij = A_ikj = -A_ijk,\n\nso every A_ijk is zero. The e_k span R^d, hence every second derivative of F is zero. On a connected open domain this makes DF a fixed orthogonal matrix Q and F-Qx a constant b. This establishes rigidity under the stated regularity and all-starting-points assumptions.",
+      "assumptions": [
+        "Bounded connected C^2 domain",
+        "C^2 diffeomorphism from D onto D",
+        "Normal Brownian covariance in the interior",
+        "Reflected law preserved for every initial point"
+      ],
+      "domain": "probability theory",
+      "source": {
+        "source_id": "owner-work:w335:p0027-smooth-map-rigidity",
+        "url": "file:///~/brain_workers/w335/P0027_proof.md",
+        "revision": "author-checked-20261010-pending-independent-w347",
+        "license": "owner-own-work",
+        "excerpt": null
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "key": "p0027-isometry-gap",
+      "namespace": "probability.stochastic-processes",
+      "logical_name": "reflected-brownian-isometry-coupling-positive-displacement-criterion",
+      "kind": "lemma",
+      "title": "Positive displacement characterizes shy isometry couplings [draft; pending w347]",
+      "statement": "Let F(x)=Qx+b be a Euclidean isometry preserving a bounded connected smooth domain D. Suppose normal reflected Brownian motion X in D almost surely visits every nonempty interior open set infinitely often. Then Y=F(X) is a reflected-Brownian coupling, and it is shy if and only if min_{closure D}|x-F(x)|>0. A bounded convex preserving domain has a fixed centroid and therefore no shy isometry coupling. A planar annulus with inner radius R>0 and nontrivial rotation theta has separation at least 2R*|sin(theta/2)|. No claim about arbitrary nondeterministic shy couplings is made.",
+      "proof_text": "For an isometry F preserving D, normal reflection is equivariant: Q sends Brownian noise to Brownian noise and the inward normal at x to the inward normal at F(x). Thus F(X) has the reflected law. Suppose also that X visits every nonempty interior open set infinitely often almost surely, as normal reflected Brownian motion does in a bounded smooth connected domain. Then the deterministic isometry coupling is shy exactly when\n\n    min_{x in closure D} |x-F(x)| > 0.\n\nIf the minimum is positive the distance stays uniformly positive. If it is zero, for every epsilon>0 the set {x in D: |x-F(x)|<epsilon} is nonempty and open by continuity and density of D in its closure. Recurrence forces visits to it. Applying this to epsilon=1/k shows inf_t |X_t-F(X_t)|=0 almost surely, ruling out shyness.\n\nIf D is convex, its volume centroid c lies in D. Any preserving Euclidean isometry fixes c: change variables in its centroid integral to obtain Qc+b=c. Therefore this regular deterministic class has no shy coupling on a bounded convex smooth domain. This is not a claim about all nondeterministic couplings on such a domain.\n\nOn the annulus R<|x|<R_out in R^2, 0<R<R_out, rotation by theta has minimum displacement 2R*|sin(theta/2)|. Every nontrivial rotation modulo 2*pi is therefore a deterministic shy coupling. The prior antipodal example is theta=pi. Letting R=0 or theta=0 removes the positive-gap check.",
+      "assumptions": [
